# Every tracked file must match a rule in this file. A file matching nothing
# fails tests/check-branch-drift.sh, which is what stops a file added on one
# branch from silently escaping the comparison.
#
# Three kinds of rule:
#   path     shared: must be byte-identical on both branches
#   !path    excluded from an enclosing shared path
#   ~path    per-branch: tracked on purpose, never compared
#
# A trailing slash means "this directory and everything under it". Without
# one, the rule matches that exact path only, so DOTFILES.md does not cover
# DOTFILES.md.bak.
#
# This file lists itself, so both branches carry identical rules.
#
# The ~ list is meant to stay near-empty. A config file with a platform
# difference is a shared base plus `-mac` / `-linux` variants that ALSO ship
# on both branches, so the variants are drift-checked too and only the
# selection differs per machine. ~/.scripts/platform.sh spells that
# convention once; every base file loads its variant through it.
#
# Reach for ~ only when a file has no meaning at all on the other platform
# and no shared part worth extracting.

# --- shared -------------------------------------------------------------

.sync-manifest
.github/workflows/
.claude/agents/
.claude/rules/
.claude/skills/
.claude/scripts/
.claude/data/
.claude/hooks/
.claude/CLAUDE.md
tests/
.scripts/
crates/
docs/research/
docs/porting-a-fix-across-branches.md
.agents/
DOTFILES.md

# Shell: one shared .zshrc, loaded variants beside it.
.zshrc
.zshrc-mac
.zshrc-linux

# Terminal multiplexer: tmux.conf sources its variant last, guarded by
# `source-file -q` so a missing one is not an error.
.config/tmux/

# Terminal emulator: alacritty.toml imports a generated pointer
# (alacritty-platform.toml, untracked) that ~/.scripts/alacritty-platform.sh
# writes from this machine's variant. Alacritty has no conditional import, so
# importing both variants directly would apply both everywhere.
.config/alacritty/

# Editor config: no platform difference at all.
.config/nvim/

# Setup notes: the platform-specific halves are their own files.
README.md
README-MAC.md
README-LINUX.md

# Login PATH for bash and sh. Shared: it names ~/.local/bin and ~/.cargo/bin,
# which are the same on both platforms, and zsh gets the same directories from
# .zshrc rather than from here. Nothing in it is platform-specific, so a
# per-branch copy would only be a second place to forget.
.profile

# The bootstrap entry point. Shared rather than per-branch on purpose: it
# detects the platform at run time and checks out the matching branch, so one
# copy has to work from either branch's raw URL. A per-branch setup.sh would
# mean the curl one-liner behaved differently depending on which branch the
# reader happened to copy it from.
setup.sh

# --- per-branch by design -----------------------------------------------

# aerospace is a macOS window manager. There is no Linux counterpart to
# share a base with, and no shared part to extract.
~.config/aerospace/

# iTerm2 is macOS-only, and this is its exported binary plist rather than
# hand-written config. Diffing it across branches would report noise from
# whichever machine last opened the app.
~.config/iterm-profiles/

# Planning docs describe code that must match; the docs themselves need not.
~docs/superpowers/
